+#### Max Consecutive Losses
+
+`MaxConsecutiveLosses` looks at trades within `lookback_period` in minutes (or in candles when using `lookback_period_candles`), ordered by close time, and counts how many of the most recent trades in a row were losses - a single winning (or break-even) trade resets the streak.
+If `trade_limit` or more losses happened back-to-back, trading will stop for `stop_duration` in minutes (or in candles when using `stop_duration_candles`, or until the set time when using `unlock_at`).
+
+This applies across all pairs, unless `only_per_pair` is set to true, which will then only look at one pair at a time.
+
+Similarly, this protection will by default look at all trades (long and short). For futures bots, setting `only_per_side` will make the bot only consider one side, and will then only lock this one side.
+
+The below example stops trading for all pairs for 12 candles if the last 4 closed trades in a row - within the last 48 candles - were all losses.
+
+``` python
+@property
+def protections(self):
+    return [
+        {
+            "method": "MaxConsecutiveLosses",
+            "lookback_period_candles": 48,
+            "trade_limit": 4,
+            "stop_duration_candles": 12,
+            "only_per_pair": False,
+            "only_per_side": False
+        }
+    ]
+```
+
+!!! Note
+    Unlike `StoplossGuard`, `MaxConsecutiveLosses` considers any losing trade (not only stoploss exits), and the streak is broken by a single winning trade in between - it is not simply a count of losses within the window.
+    `trade_limit` and `lookback_period` will need to be tuned for your strategy - `lookback_period` must be long enough to contain the full losing streak you want to detect.
+
 #### Cooldown Period
 
 `CooldownPeriod` locks a pair for `stop_duration` in minutes (or in candles when using `stop_duration_candles`, or until the set time when using `unlock_at`) after exiting, avoiding a re-entry for this pair for `stop_duration` minutes.
